course: language
lesson: 33
title: Ukrainian compared with English
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ukrainian and English are distant cousins in the same huge language family, but they solve the same jobs in different ways, like two cooks making soup from different recipes.
explain: English shows who does what mostly by word order; Ukrainian shows it mostly with word endings, so Danylo can say "Собака бачить кота" or "Кота бачить собака" and both mean "the dog sees the cat".
explain: When Mia hears her friend shout "Мамо!" across the playground, not "Мама!", she has met a word ending that exists just for calling someone.
explain: Ukrainian is written in the Cyrillic alphabet of 33 letters, some of which look like English letters but stand for other sounds, and some of which belong to Ukrainian alone.
explain: Neither way is better: English uses order and small words like "a", "the", "to" and "with", Ukrainian uses endings, and both get the meaning across.
fact: Ukrainian uses the Cyrillic alphabet with 33 letters; some look like English letters but sound different: "В" sounds like v, "Н" like n, "Р" like r.
fact: Ukrainian has no words for "a" or "the" (no articles); "Кіт спить" can mean "a cat sleeps" or "the cat sleeps", and the situation shows which.
fact: Ukrainian nouns change their endings to show their job in the sentence: "мама" (mum), "мами" (of mum), "мамі" (to mum), "маму" (mum, as the object), "мамою" (with mum), "на мамі" (on mum), "мамо!" (calling Mum); these forms are called cases, and Ukrainian has seven.
fact: English mostly uses word order and small words instead of cases, but pronouns like he, him and his still change a little.
fact: The calling form is called the vocative: "Мамо!", "Тарасе!" for Taras, "Олено!" for Olena; Old English had a calling form too but lost it.
fact: Because endings show who does what, Ukrainian word order is freer: "Кота бачить собака" still means "the dog sees the cat".
fact: Ukrainian has two words for "you": "ти" for friends and family, and "ви" for teachers, strangers or several people; old English had "thou" and "you" in a similar way.
fact: "Kiev" came from the Russian spelling; "Kyiv" follows Ukrainian, and after the #KyivNotKiev campaign started in 2018, many governments, airports and newspapers switched to "Kyiv".
fact: Ukrainian nouns have grammatical gender, a grammar label, not boy or girl: "стіл" (table) is masculine, "книга" (book) feminine, "вікно" (window) neuter.
fact: "Дякую" (DYAH-koo-yu) means thank you, "будь ласка" means please or you're welcome, and "привіт" means hi.
fact: Among the major Cyrillic alphabets, the letter "ї" (sounds like "yi", as in "Україна") is used only in Ukrainian and the closely related Rusyn; "ґ" (a hard g) is another special Ukrainian letter.
exercise: Mykola's friend says the Ukrainian alphabet is "English with weird letters". What is it really called? | Cyrillic | Latin | Greek | Runic
exercise: How many letters does the Ukrainian alphabet have? | 33 | 26 | 24 | 40
exercise: What sound does the Ukrainian letter "Р" make? | r | p | b | f
exercise: Sophie asks why Ukrainian has no "a" or "the". How does Ukrainian manage? | The situation shows whether a thing is new or known | Ukrainians use a little "the" sound that English ears can't hear | Ukrainian uses "a" but hides it | Every noun is always "the"
exercise: Olena says "мама", "мами", "мамі", "маму". What are these forms called? | Cases | Genders | Tenses | Articles
exercise: How does English mostly show a word's job in the sentence? | Word order and small words like "to" and "with" | Seven case endings on every noun, just like Ukrainian | Tones of voice | Capital letters at the start of important words
exercise: Mia hears "Мамо!" not "Мама!". What is this form called? | The vocative, for calling someone | The plural, for more than one mum | A spelling mistake made by children | The past tense
exercise: How would you call your friend Taras across the playground in Ukrainian? | Тарасе! | Тараса! | Тарасом! | Тарасові!
exercise: Danylo says "Кота бачить собака". Who sees whom? | The dog sees the cat | The cat sees the dog | They see each other | Nobody sees anyone
exercise: Why can Ukrainian word order move around more freely than English? | The endings show who does what | Ukrainians don't care about meaning | Ukrainian has no verbs | All Ukrainian words are short
exercise: Katya would use "ви" with whom? | Her teacher | Her little brother | Her best friend | Her cat
exercise: Which spelling of Ukraine's capital follows Ukrainian? | Kyiv | Kiev | Kieff | Kijev
exercise: Andriy says "стіл" (table) is masculine. What does that mean? | It's a grammar label that changes endings | Tables are boys | Only men can use tables | It's a big table
exercise: What does "дякую" mean? | Thank you | Hello | Goodbye | Please
exercise: Which letter is special to Ukrainian among the major Cyrillic alphabets? | Ї | А | О | М
exercise: What does "привіт" mean? | Hi | Thank you | Sorry | Goodnight
test: Your mate goes "Ukrainian is just backwards English, bruv." What's true? | It's a separate language with its own alphabet and grammar | He's right, it's English written backwards in funny-looking letters | It's a kind of Russian | It's English with extra letters only
test: Sophie's mate says "every language needs a and the, innit." What's true? | Ukrainian manages without articles, using the situation | He's right, every language on Earth has words for "a" and "the" | Ukrainian uses "the" but spells it differently | Only English and Ukrainian have no articles
test: What would happen if you swapped the words in "The dog sees the cat" in English? | The meaning would change: the cat sees the dog | Nothing would change, just like in the Ukrainian sentence | It would become a question | It would become nonsense in every case
test: A Ukrainian girl calls "Олено!" and an English boy says "she said the name wrong". What's true? | It's the vocative, the normal form for calling Olena | He's right, it's a mistake | It's a different name | It's the plural of Olena
test: Your Yorkshire grandad says "eh up, ah'm thee mate, call me thee." ("Eh up" means hello; "thee" is an old word for you still heard in parts of Yorkshire.) Which Ukrainian "you" is like his friendly "thee"? | Ти | Ви | Вони | Ми
test: A newspaper writes "Kiev" in 2026. What's the better spelling, and why? | Kyiv, because it follows Ukrainian | Kiev, because it's the older English spelling | Kiyv, because it's shorter | It doesn't matter at all
test: Your mate laughs "so in Ukrainian a window is neither boy nor girl, lol, it's an IT." What's true? | "Вікно" is neuter, a grammar label, not a real "it-person" | Windows really have a gender | It's a mistake in Ukrainian | Only English has neuter
test: A Gen Z TikToker says "дякую is lowkey impossible to say, I'm not even gonna try." ("Lowkey" means a bit.) What's good advice? | Try "DYAH-koo-yu"; a few polite words show respect | You must be fluent in Ukrainian before you try saying any word | It's rude to try | Just say "thank you" louder
test: Your cousin says "all Cyrillic alphabets are the same, so Ukrainian and Russian letters match exactly." What shows she's wrong? | Ukrainian has its own letters like ї and ґ | Cyrillic has only 10 letters | Russian has no letters | Ukrainian uses Latin letters
test: Ivan writes "Україна". Which letter here is the special Ukrainian "yi"? | Ї | У | К | Н
test: Your English friend says "English has no cases at all, mate." What's a fair reply? | Mostly true, but pronouns like he, him and his still change | Totally true, English never changes the ending of any word at all | English has seven cases like Ukrainian | English has more cases than Ukrainian
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means by which Ukrainian indicates the grammatical function of a noun? | Principally by the alteration of word endings, termed cases | Principally by fixed word order identical to that of English | Principally by tones of voice | Principally by the use of articles
exam: Identify the meaning of the Ukrainian sentence "Кота бачить собака". | The dog sees the cat | The cat sees the dog | The cat and the dog see each other | The dog does not see the cat
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vocative case in Ukrainian. | It is the form employed in calling or addressing a person, as in "Мамо!" | It is the form employed exclusively in written letters and official correspondence | It is an error committed by young speakers | It denotes the plural number
exam: In respect of the pronouns "ти" and "ви", ascertain the circumstance in which "ви" is appropriately employed. | In addressing a teacher, a stranger or several persons | In addressing a younger sibling | In addressing oneself | In addressing a domestic animal
